ual to 3 which means there  are three cluster generativity in this  data set cluster STD it's a standard  deviation of each course in cluster the  random stage parameter is just the  random state for the data preparation we  can reproduce the data generation  process we sustain random States setting  great now let's have a look at the  generated x and y access 200 samples in  8 there are two columns in the data set  each columns represent a feature for  that sample why it's just the list of  clusters labels for each sample as  expected both of them has 200 samples in  them  now let's try to visualize the data set  in the pot here we are using scatter  function from madpal leap to visualize  the data set in two dimension if you  have more than two features you can use  some dimension reduction techniques like  PCA to reduce the features into two  dimension and then visualize it in a two  dimensional space from the plot you may  find that there are a lot of overlapping  between different clusters and there are  no clear boundary between them the  reason for that is that we have pets the  Gaussian standard deviation has one  which is a relatively large standard  deviation here I encourage you to  experiment with the standard deviation  parameter offline trying to reduce it to  a smaller value and you will find a  clear boundary between different  clusters cool let's move - how to train  a kemi's model with this dataset here we  are using camis class in s : a zero  let's have a closer look at the  parameters in this class first we have n  clusters set as 3 and cluster is the  number of clusters to form as well at  the number of centers to generate this  is one of the most important  hyperparameters for this model and we  will tell you how to choose this number  zone in each defines the method for  initialization here we set it as a  random in this case we choose 3  observation at random from the data set  for the initial centers n 8 is the  number of times that k-means 